# The Ministry of Herbology — every variable, names and shapes only.
#
# There is not one usable value in this file and there never will be. A guide
# that hands out a working password is how a self-hosted application ends up
# with ten thousand installations sharing one credential. Where a
# value is needed, the comment says how to generate your own.
#
# Two files come out of this one:
#
#   .env local development. `cp .env.example .env` and it works as-is,
#                 because MOH_MOCK_MODE=true serves fixtures and nothing real
#                 is contacted. No credential is needed to run the dev stack.
#
#   .env.deploy your deployment. Copy the "Deployment" section below into it
#                 and fill it in. `make stack-deploy` reads it. Both files are
#                 in .gitignore; keep it that way.
#
# Credentials are NOT in either file. Anything that grants access is a Docker
# swarm secret, created with `docker secret create` and mounted as a file
#. What you see below are the non-secret halves: hostnames,
# usernames, URLs and sizes.


# ==============================================================================
# Local development
# ==============================================================================

# Fixtures instead of the world. The dev stack runs offline on this.
MOH_MOCK_MODE=true
MOH_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://herbology:herbology@db:5432/herbology
MOH_REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
MOH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# The dev broker is anonymous (infra/compose/docker-compose.dev.yml runs
# mosquitto with -c /mosquitto-no-auth.conf). The deployed one is not.
MOH_MQTT_HOST=mosquitto
MOH_MQTT_PORT=1883
MOH_MQTT_USERNAME=
MOH_MQTT_PASSWORD=

# Home Assistant. Empty means "not configured", which is a
# perfectly good answer; the Ministry Office says so rather than pretending.
MOH_HA_BASE_URL=
MOH_HA_TOKEN=

# A Home Assistant notify service — the name of one, e.g. the mobile_app
# service for a phone — used for members whose own notify_prefs name none.
# Empty means nobody is set up for notifications, which the Ministry Office
# reports as a setup step rather than a fault. Not a credential: the token is
# MOH_HA_TOKEN above (and the moh_ha_token secret when deployed).
#MOH_HA_NOTIFY_SERVICE=

# Where the hub finds this deployment's own API, to ask what is due and
# whether a frost is coming — in the hub worker and in the API's own process,
# which serves the Lunette. Both stacks set it on the api, web and worker-hub
# services to the API's internal address, so you set it only when running
# outside Docker, and it then points at wherever your API is listening. No
# credential travels on it.
#MOH_API_URL=

# The three image directories (the maps module, the journal and the inventory API). None has a default, on
# purpose: unset, plan and photo uploads are refused with a 503 naming the
# variable and the Journal reports every plate as store_unavailable, rather
# than writing into a container layer that the next deploy throws away. Both
# stacks set all three to the mount point of a named volume, so inside Docker
# you never set them. Outside Docker, each is an absolute path to a directory
# that survives — one you choose, and never one inside this repository.
#MOH_GROUNDS_IMAGE_DIR=
#MOH_PLATES_IMAGE_DIR=
#MOH_PHOTOS_IMAGE_DIR=

# Weather. Open-Meteo needs no key; the US National Weather Service refuses
# traffic whose User-Agent carries no contact address. Put your own in.
MOH_NWS_USER_AGENT=ministry-of-herbology (contact@example.invalid)

# Optional, gated on open decision 1
MOH_PERENUAL_API_KEY=
MOH_PLANTNET_API_KEY=


# ==============================================================================
# Deployment — copy this section into .env.deploy and fill it in
# ==============================================================================
# `docker stack deploy` interpolates from the shell and has no --env-file, so
# `make stack-deploy` sources this file for you. docs/deploy/README.md is the
# walkthrough; this is the reference.

# --- Required. The deploy fails, loudly and by name, without each of these. ---

# Your registry and namespace, no trailing slash. Any registry: a private
# Harbor, ghcr.io, a plain `registry:2` on the same box. The images are named
# moh-api, moh-web and moh-worker underneath it.
#   MOH_REGISTRY=registry.example.net/ministry-of-herbology

# The tag to run. `make push` prints the one it built, derived from the commit.
# Pin it — `latest` makes a rollback impossible to describe.
#   MOH_IMAGE_TAG=

# The https:// URL the app is reached on, with no trailing slash. It goes into
# the ICS calendar feed URLs and the PWA manifest, so getting it wrong is
# visible later rather than immediately.
#   MOH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://herbology.example.net

# A contact address, in the shape below. The US National Weather Service
# refuses traffic without one and is entitled to.
#   MOH_NWS_USER_AGENT=ministry-of-herbology (you@example.net)

# --- Optional. Sensible defaults shown; none of them is a credential. ---

# Database role and database name. Changing these after the first deploy means
# the volume no longer matches; pick them once.
#MOH_DB_USER=herbology
#MOH_DB_NAME=herbology

# The MQTT username the API and the hub worker log in as. Its password is the
# moh_mqtt_password secret, and the broker's copy is moh_mqtt_passwords.
#MOH_MQTT_USERNAME=herbology

# Home Assistant's base URL, e.g. http://homeassistant.local:8123 — the token
# beside it is the moh_ha_token secret, never a variable. Leave unset if you
# have no Home Assistant; the app runs without one.
#MOH_HA_BASE_URL=

# The Home Assistant notify service to use for a member who has not chosen
# one — the one phone in a one-phone household. A service name, not a
# credential. Unset, nobody is notified and the Ministry Office says so.
#MOH_HA_NOTIFY_SERVICE=

# Not in this section, and not for you to set: MOH_API_URL and the three image
# directories, MOH_GROUNDS_IMAGE_DIR, MOH_PLATES_IMAGE_DIR and
# MOH_PHOTOS_IMAGE_DIR. The stack fixes each to an address or a mount point of
# its own. The images live on the moh_grounds-images, moh_plates-images and
# moh_photos-images volumes, which `make backup` archives with the database —
# README §13.

# Which swarm secret stands behind each of the six, by name. Unset, each is
# the secret's own name (moh_db_password and so on), which is what §6 creates.
# Set one only to rotate it: a secret cannot be edited, so the replacement goes
# in under a new name and this line tells every later deploy to use it
# (docs/deploy/README.md §12, Rotating a secret). Names, never values.
#MOH_DB_PASSWORD_SECRET=
#MOH_MQTT_PASSWORD_SECRET=
#MOH_MQTT_PASSWORDS_SECRET=
#MOH_HA_TOKEN_SECRET=
#MOH_TLS_CERT_SECRET=
#MOH_TLS_KEY_SECRET=

# How services find each other. vip, the swarm default, needs the kernel's
# ip_vs module; on a host without it (some VPS and container-optimised images)
# service names resolve and then time out. dnsrr needs no IPVS.
# docs/deploy/README.md §15.
#MOH_ENDPOINT_MODE=vip

# Ports on the node. Change them if something else already holds 80 and 443,
# and remember that an ACME HTTP-01 challenge needs to reach port 80.
#MOH_HTTP_PORT=80
#MOH_HTTPS_PORT=443
#MOH_MQTT_PORT=1883

# The IANA zone the container clocks report. Everything is stored UTC
# regardless (see the project conventions); this only affects log timestamps.
#MOH_TZ=UTC

# Replica counts. The database, the broker and each worker are deliberately not
# in this list: they are 1 by design, and two Arq schedulers means two waterings.
#MOH_API_REPLICAS=2
#MOH_WEB_REPLICAS=2

# Resource limits. Defaults suit a small home server; raise the database's
# memory first if the Almanac's history queries feel slow.
#MOH_DB_CPU_LIMIT=2.0
#MOH_DB_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g
#MOH_DB_MEMORY_RESERVATION=512m
#MOH_REDIS_CPU_LIMIT=0.5
#MOH_REDIS_MEMORY_LIMIT=384m
#MOH_REDIS_MAXMEMORY=256mb
#MOH_MQTT_CPU_LIMIT=0.5
#MOH_MQTT_MEMORY_LIMIT=128m
#MOH_API_CPU_LIMIT=1.0
#MOH_API_MEMORY_LIMIT=768m
#MOH_API_MEMORY_RESERVATION=192m
#MOH_WEB_CPU_LIMIT=1.0
#MOH_WEB_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
#MOH_WEB_MEMORY_RESERVATION=128m
#MOH_WORKER_CPU_LIMIT=0.75
#MOH_WORKER_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
#MOH_WORKER_MEMORY_RESERVATION=128m
#MOH_NGINX_CPU_LIMIT=0.5
#MOH_NGINX_MEMORY_LIMIT=192m

# Bump this whenever you edit infra/stack/nginx/*.conf or the mosquitto config.
# Swarm config objects are immutable: without a new name, the old content keeps
# being served and nothing tells you.
#MOH_CONFIG_VERSION=1

# The stack name. Everything else keys off it, including scripts/migrate.sh
# and scripts/backup.sh finding the database.
#MOH_STACK=moh


# ==============================================================================
# The six secrets — created with `docker secret create`, never written here
# ==============================================================================
#
#   moh_db_password the Postgres password for MOH_DB_USER
#   moh_mqtt_password the broker password for MOH_MQTT_USERNAME
#   moh_mqtt_passwords the broker's own password file, from mosquitto_passwd
#   moh_ha_token a Home Assistant long-lived access token
#   moh_tls_cert your certificate chain, PEM
#   moh_tls_key its private key, PEM
#
# docs/deploy/README.md gives the exact command for each, including how to
# generate the ones you invent and what to do about the ones you do not have.
